[17592186341881 1111 #uuid "8e7b45c5-e703-5e0e-a883-0517508d7314" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341882 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341883 13194139535289 true] [17592186341882 1089 "q-39246" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341882 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535289 true] [17592186341882 1100 "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -7 \\\\ -7 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341882 1113 #uuid "dbc853f4-0a98-5ff6-ba34-a621d867ba7f" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341882 1145 "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]$  is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the main diagonal are not the same.\n\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -7 \\\\ -7 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the main diagonal are not the same.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341884 13194139535289 true] [17592186341883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341885 13194139535289 true] [17592186341883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341886 13194139535289 true] [17592186341883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341887 13194139535289 true] [17592186341883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341888 13194139535289 true] [17592186341883 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535289 true] [17592186341883 1041 "selection" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341883 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341886 13194139535289 true] [17592186341883 1110 #uuid "d1522444-abd2-5216-978b-cc1f9e611a0e" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341884 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341884 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535289 true] [17592186341884 1111 #uuid "5e886bb9-d24b-5682-86ef-b6604d6ccbb5" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341885 1034 "III only" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341885 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535289 true] [17592186341885 1111 #uuid "9a482c8b-0c96-5b77-bae5-e39afaec9ecb" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341886 1034 "None" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341886 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535289 true] [17592186341886 1111 #uuid "b2b90bc5-52d7-5243-8d4a-68892b08fc7f" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341887 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341887 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535289 true] [17592186341887 1111 #uuid "173218a6-87a1-5645-b673-04c41d26f9d6" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341888 1034 "II only" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341888 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535289 true] [17592186341888 1111 #uuid "9a54b26c-28b9-511c-93e9-81250b9ce393" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341889 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341890 13194139535289 true] [17592186341889 1089 "q-39247" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341889 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535289 true] [17592186341889 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061787/assets/88be779d85ba51746abba7a71cad58b6986fd72a14ba4fc09e8b015494f57c0e.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341889 1113 #uuid "29dc5248-66c2-57d6-8e17-1a6c4f6d7425" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341889 1145 "Notice that $A$ is a rotation-scaling matrix. So, we need to find its scale factor and the angle of rotation.\n\nWe can find the scale factor using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{det(A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent $A$ as a product of a rotation and a scaling matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ clockwise by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$  and scale the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061787/assets/f5f7e7fa8d149ae9b6f07af57cbc8fc103b2d7c306aec98dbb2119145aa276ca.png)" 13194139535289 true] [17592186341890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341891 13194139535289 true] [17592186341890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341892 13194139535289 true] [17592186341890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341893 13194139535289 true] [17592186341890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341894 13194139535289 true] [17592186341890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341895 13194139535289 true] [17592186341890 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535289 true] [17592186341890 1041 "selection" 13194139535289 true] [17592186